erset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482549 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72a8e42a-c396-5007-82f3-4a7623931a98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482549 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482586 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbdebe64-e032-577f-8298-161c9dc3fe78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482586 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n