} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482611 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482611 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482611 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f16e5a65-b3a9-5e9e-afd2-4d31b6ccc499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482611 #edb/ref 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
